# Line-ending normalization.
#
# Development happens on Windows; CI and production run Linux. Without this file,
# behaviour depends on each machine's `core.autocrlf` setting — which is `true`
# on this dev machine, meaning git rewrites line endings on checkout and commit.
# That is invisible until something downstream cares:
#
#   * a `run: |` block in a GitHub workflow with CRLF fails in bash with
#     `$'\r': command not found`
#   * shell scripts become unexecutable
#   * diffs show whole files as changed after a checkout on another platform
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` stores everything as LF in the repository and checks it out
# as LF everywhere, so the repo contents are identical on every machine
# regardless of local git config.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicitly LF — these are read by Linux tooling and break subtly otherwise.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.sql       text eol=lf
*.mjs       text eol=lf
*.js        text eol=lf
*.ts        text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf
.node-version   text eol=lf
.npmrc          text eol=lf
Dockerfile*     text eol=lf

# Binary — never touch these.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.gz    binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.node  binary
*.wasm  binary

# Generated — collapse in diffs and exclude from language stats.
pnpm-lock.yaml  linguist-generated=true -diff
